WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
- Not that long ago, we
were a tiny little startup

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.240
with a crazy idea

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.730
and here we are getting
ready to go public, so.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.380
- [Narrator] That was Coinbases' CEO,

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.790
just a few weeks before the company

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:13.740
scored an $85 billion valuation
in its stock market debut.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.960
Since it was founded in 2012,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.730
the company has grown to be the largest

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.890
crypto currency exchange in the US,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.190
thanks in part to its focus
on making crypto accessible.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.410
- It's just going to be sort of an easy,

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.290
seamless way for people
to just get into crypto

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.140
and trade it, and that was their idea.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.370
- [Narrator] It helped
the company bring in

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:36.690
record revenue in 2021 as more
traders joined the platform.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.420
But as bitcoin and other
crypto currencies fell

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.970
Coinbases' share value fell along with it

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.410
exposing deeper issues

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.870
with its business practices and culture.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.703
So, what went wrong?

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.890
In 2021 the crypto industry was booming.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.770
- The Coinbase public listing on its own

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.160
was driving a lot of enthusiasm
for the rest of the market.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.070
This was seen as a bellwether news event.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.470
- [Narrator] The explosion
in crypto drove more people

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.950
including many retail traders
to Coinbases' platform,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.470
and that drove up revenue in a big way.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.110
Most of the company's money
comes from trading fees

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.630
which it charges two types of traders.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.070
First, retail.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.560
- Who are basically,
you know, mom and pop,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.720
young kids, people like you and me,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.270
people just kind of doing
this out there on their own.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.310
- [Narrator] And second, institutional.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.260
- Brokerage houses,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.550
pension funds,

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:30.390
hedge funds.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.040
I mean, people who are out there

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.290
who have a business that is predicated

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:38.130
on your trading assets
in the capital market.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.720
- [Narrator] Much of
Coinbase's trading volume

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.580
comes from these traders

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.190
but most of their revenue
comes from these ones,

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.200
who make up its core customer base.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.717
In the second quarter of
2021 90% of Coinbases'

00:01:49.717 --> 00:01:51.480
$2 billion net revenue

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.210
was made from retail transactions alone.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.340
Spurred on by a growing user base

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.440
Coinbase set its sites on growth.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.850
They beefed up on hiring,

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.470
aiming to catch up to competitors

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.320
that had a wider span of
crypto assets and products.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.600
Between the end of the
first and third quarters

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.240
it added more than a thousand employees.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.340
In October Coinbase
announced it would launch

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.230
its own NFT marketplace.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.870
And in November, the share
price shot to a high point

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.910
as the value of bitcoin peaked.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.560
But there was trouble ahead,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.160
especially for a company that
relied on crypto enthusiasm.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.980
- These things are all
latched, very, very tightly

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.020
to sentiment in the crypto markets.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.900
In the stock market stocks like Coinbase

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.300
become a proxy for the crypto markets

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.010
and that's why you see the stock prices

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.260
track crypto so closely.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.780
- [Narrator] As the Fed began
to shift its monetary policy

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.250
to tackle inflation,

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.110
many professional investors started

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.990
to pull out of assets that
they considered riskier.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:50.460
- Crypto became sort of one
asset in a larger portfolio.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.990
If you're a professional trader,

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.760
you make, you know, you buy bonds,

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.560
you buy stocks, you buy commodities,

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.290
you buy currencies, and
now you are buying crypto.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.770
So you were slotting crypto in
among those different assets

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.900
and where it ended up being slotted

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.210
for most of them was
it was the risk asset,

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.740
it was a highly volatile asset.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.380
- [Narrator] In April Coinbases'
share price drops steeply

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.700
as trading volume fell

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.610
and analysts slashed
estimates for earnings.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.920
Transactions were down and retail traders

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.840
were pulling out of the market.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.830
Some were HODL-ing shorthand
for holding on for dear life.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.560
It means that they were sitting
on their crypto currencies

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.480
trying to ride out price declines,

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.240
and hoping to come out
on top at the other end.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.380
That also means that they were making

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.300
fewer trades and transactions,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.000
the main things that Coinbase
relies on to make money.

00:03:36.841 --> 00:03:39.180
The stock price continued to slide in May

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.850
when the company reported
losses in the first quarter.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.430
- When you started to see
business results come in

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.890
and the business results were really bad.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.500
I think that's when
people started to realize

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.870
that this was not just one of,

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:56.370
you know, cryptos sort of
periodic common spasms.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.250
- [Narrator] As trading continued
to slow and revenue fell

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.310
larger problems within
the company were exposed.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.710
- Certainly early in 2022,
it was hard to tell how

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.690
severe the sell off was going to be.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.070
There wasn't a lot of panic

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:10.200
and you saw companies like Coinbase,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.860
they kept hiring, they
were in expansion mode

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.510
and they just kept running with that

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.090
despite the fact that the
prices started to fall.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.320
- [Narrator] The company hadn't built up

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.460
other streams of revenue

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.950
in time to make up for falling profits.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.380
The NFT marketplace they
had announced in October

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.810
didn't materialize until April,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.300
just as the NFT market was flat lining.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.120
The platform did just 4.2
million in transactions

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.770
between when it went live in April

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.540
and the beginning of August.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.130
Coinbase has also offered things

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.500
like staking and custodial services.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.770
- Now, especially it's
just a really hard time

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.980
to try to build new
businesses in the middle of,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.620
you know, a completely downed market.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.330
- [Narrator] In early June, a petition,

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.550
purportedly from Coinbase employees

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.860
was posted to another crypto site.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.840
It called to oust several executives

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.580
over the company's performance.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.170
Armstrong tweeted that the petition

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.600
was really dumb on multiple levels.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.510
- I think probably issues
that were inside the company

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:08.310
that largely were papered over

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.403
because times were so good in 2021

00:05:10.403 --> 00:05:12.930
started to emerge in 2022.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:13.830
- [Narrator] A few weeks later

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.020
the company announced it
would cut its workforce

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.810
by about 18% to reduce spending,

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.147
its employee count had
grown to nearly 5,000.

00:05:21.147 --> 00:05:24.150
And Coinbase ran into more trouble in July

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:25.620
as federal authorities charged

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.480
a former product manager from the company

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.130
with insider trading.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.320
Lawyers for the former
product manager said

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.330
that the charges were meritless.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.160
The SEC also took the position

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.600
that several of the crypto assets

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.160
traded on the company's platform

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.410
are classified as securities.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.990
The company, which isn't
licensed to operate

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.383
as a securities exchange,
denied that the assets qualify.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:48.690
By the end of quarter two,

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.420
the retail trading volume fell by 74%

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.780
from the high in quarter four of 2021.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.730
To make up for lost ground Coinbase plans

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.760
to continue to grow its
other streams of revenue.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.950
In June, it launched a derivatives product

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:03.990
that allows traders to bet on the price

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:05.610
of an underlying asset.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:06.960
The company announced a partnership

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.570
with Money Management
from BlackRock in August,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:10.890
which could help increase the number

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:13.800
of institutional investors
that use the platform.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.510
Coinbase says that it has built up

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:17.280
more client requested products

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.590
and is seeing a strong uptake.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.780
Armstrong told CNBC in August

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.120
that the company's planning
on increasing its earnings

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.710
from subscriptions and services,

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:29.280
which made up about 18% of
its quarter two revenue.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.660
- We're realizing that trading fees

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.790
is not gonna be that thing that,

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:33.623
you know, it's gonna be,

00:06:33.623 --> 00:06:34.830
it's still gonna be a
major part of our business

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.810
in 10 years from now,
even 20 years from now.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:38.910
But I'd like to get to a
place where more than 50%

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.580
of our revenue is
subscription and services.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.740
- [Narrator] Businesses
across the crypto industry

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.780
have been impacted by the downturn,

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.030
but competitors like Binance and FTX

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:49.620
have continued to grow

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.290
and are gaining market
share of spot trading.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:54.360
However, since neither company is public,

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:55.800
it's not clear how much their earnings

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.680
have currently been
impacted by the market.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.570
In its quarter two letter to shareholders,

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.610
Coinbase said that it
lives by the mantra -

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.110
it's never as good as it seems,

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.150
and it's never as bad as it seems.

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.070
And that, it is an all-weather company

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:11.910
with experience in navigating
crypto asset price cycles.

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.320
The company's share price has seen

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.303
a small increase in recent months.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:18.930
Coinbase says that it continues
to take a long term view

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.663
and remains focused on
building for the future.

